  "response": "## The sweep is one finding, not a set\n\n`crimes scan --format json` reports 39 findings across this fixture. Exactly **one** has `\"type\": \"hardcoded_local_path\"` — `crime_00018`. I grepped the working tree to confirm the scan wasn't under-reporting: the only absolute filesystem path literal in `src/` is that same line. The other `\"/…\"` literals (`src/nav/sidebar.ts:5`, `src/nav/registry.ts:5`) are route paths, not disk paths.\n\nSo the \"portability sweep\" is a one-line change, and the interesting part is which anchor it gets.\n\n### crime_00018 — `src/api/files.ts:9`\n\nLiteral from the finding's evidence: **`` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9 ``**\n\n```ts\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = \"/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json\";\n```\n\nRead at runtime by `loadPlanSchema()` via `existsSync` / `statSync` / `readFileSync`.\n\n**Anchor: none of the three, quite.** The recommendation is *vendor the file, then resolve from the repo root*, with an env override. Reasoning for each option:\n\n- **`os.homedir()` — reject.** This is the detector's default suggestion (`suggested_actions[0].kind: \"use_portable_home_path\"`), and it is wrong here. Rewriting to `join(homedir(), \"dev/crimes/schema.json\")` keeps the breakage and hides it: it encodes the claim \"every machine checks out the `crimes` repo at `~/dev/crimes`\". That's true on exactly one laptop. CI runners have no such checkout and no meaningful `$HOME` layout. `os.homedir()` is the right anchor when the home directory *is* the natural location — `~/.config/app/`, `~/.cache/`, a user-scoped credential file. It is not the right anchor for someone's project workspace.\n\n- **Relative to repo root — primary.** The literal points at `schema.json` in the **parent `crimes` repo**, not in this one. This package's root is `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app`; the target sits four levels above it. So you can't write a clean relative path today — `../../../../schema.json` would escape the package and break the moment it's installed from a registry rather than run from a checkout. A schema the code parses at runtime is a **build asset and belongs in the repo**. Copy it to `src/schema.json` (or `assets/schema.json`, added to `package.json#files`), then:\n\n  ```ts\n  import { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n  const DEFAULT_SCHEMA_PATH = fileURLToPath(new URL(\"../schema.json\", import.meta.url));\n  ```\n\n  Module-relative, not cwd-relative — this keeps working when the CLI (`package.json` declares `bin: messy`) is invoked from an arbitrary directory. Use `path.join(__dirname, \"../schema.json\")` if the package is CJS.\n\n- **`process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` — secondary, as an override only.** Worth adding because the current code silently returns `null` when the file is missing (`if (!existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)) return null;`), so a deploy that needs a different schema has no way to supply one and no way to learn it didn't. Make it an override on top of the vendored default, not the sole source:\n\n  ```ts\n  const SCHEMA_PATH = process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH ?? DEFAULT_SCHEMA_PATH;\n  ```\n\n  If you make the env var the *only* source, you've converted a broken-on-every-machine-but-one bug into a broken-until-configured bug — strictly better, but the vendored default costs nothing and makes the common path work unconfigured.\n\n**Effort/risk:** the finding says `\"effort\": \"quick\"`, `\"risk\": \"low\"`. That's right for the code edit, but the vendoring step means copying a file and adding it to the package manifest — still small, just not a single-line diff.\n\n### Two things worth flagging\n\n**Adjacent, same file, not in scope of your ask.** `crime_00017` (`hardcoded_localhost`, `\"medium\"`) is `src/api/files.ts:8` — evidence `` `localhost:3000` @L8 ``, the line immediately above. Different detector, so it's not a `hardcoded_local_path` finding, but it's the same class of defect in the same four lines and the file's own header comment calls out both. Fixing one and leaving the other would be odd; the URL wants `process.env.ADMIN_URL` with a dev default, since unlike the schema there's no in-repo artifact to anchor to.\n\n**One completeness caveat on the enumeration.** The scan's `coverage.warnings` reports `.crimes/suppressions.json` matched an exclude pattern and was never read — \"findings in it are absent, not zero.\" It's a suppressions config, so a source path there is unlikely, but the sweep's \"every path\" claim holds over scanned files only. Separately, this detector matches three shapes (`/Users/<name>/`, `/home/<name>/`, `C:\\Users\\<name>\\`); a non-portable absolute path like `/opt/data/schema.json` wouldn't be flagged by it. My grep covered that broader shape and found nothing else.",
